  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 247 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\n(Sunday - Monday Night)\n\nDecreasing clouds are forecast from west to east on Sunday as the \nremnants of Hurricane Isaias moves toward the mid Atlantic. \nTemperatures will begin to rebound with widespread 80s areawide. A \nfew locations in central Missouri may top out at the 90 degree mark. \nThe warmest day of the next week is still likely to be on Monday. \nThis is when mid/upper level ridging is centered across the \nMississippi Valley, with 850-hPa temperatures nearing +20C (near-\nrecord for the date). Winds also veer more to the southwest, which \ndownslope off of the Ozark Plateau. There also isn't much of a \nsignal for any cloud cover. In other words, the ingredients are all \nthere for a day of record-breaking warmth. Most locations are \nforecast to reach the low 90s, with a few spots potentially in the \nmid 90s in central/east central Missouri due to the downsloping. All \nthree daily record highs from St. Louis (90F/1924), Columbia \n(94F/1899), and Quincy (88F/1975) are in jeopardy. For St. Louis, \nthe all-time record warmest October temperature (94F on 10/11/1963) \nmay also be threatened. \n\n(Tuesday - Next Friday)\n\nA midlevel shortwave trough is expected to transverse the Great \nLakes early next week, with a surface low downstream across the \nnortheast. A cold front will be draped to its southwest and move \ntoward the mid-Mississippi Valley late Monday night into Tuesday \nbefore stalling. The timing and evolution of this front remains \nhighly uncertain, with large run-to-run variability on deterministic \nguidance. Overall, we should see at least a slight \"cooling\" trend \nfor midweek, as at the minimum, a substantial increase in clouds are \nexpected in the vicinity of the boundary. Chances of showers and \nthunderstorms will also increase, which would also limit the diurnal \nrise in temperatures. Speaking of which, it does look like a wet \nperiod of time regionally, with multiple rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms focused mainly along/north of the quasi-stationary \nfront. Rainfall anomalies over the 5-day period ending next Friday \non both the GEFS and EPS show surpluses of 0.25-0.75\" from southeast \nto northwest across our region. \n\nClusters of the 500-hPa height pattern show a stronger trough \ndigging into the Great Lakes. There is however a considerable amount \nof spread, both in terms of timing but also the strength of the air \nmass advecting southward out of Canada. Two of the four clusters \n(~47% of LREF members) have a very strong trough, which would bring \nin the coolest air so far this Fall by a considerable margin (think \nhighs in 50s, lows in 30s potentially). On the other side of the \nspectrum, the other two clusters show a much more modest trough \nswinging through the Great Lakes. There is still a clear cold \nfrontal passage between Thursday and next Friday, but temperatures \nonly drop back closer to, or slightly above, normal.",
